Grant READ_IMPLIES_EXEC lazily, per page, for a 32-bit guest.

Linux sets the READ_IMPLIES_EXEC personality for an i386 ELF with no
PT_GNU_STACK (fs/binfmt_elf.c), making readable memory executable; pre-NX
hardware behaved that way regardless. Megatouch Maxx Ruby has no PT_GNU_STACK
and loads each game module into malloc'd memory and jumps into it, so CheckExec
refuses and synthesises a SIGSEGV the guest does not handle:

    Emit Signal 11 at IP=0x9884bb8 / addr=0x9884bb8, code=0xecec
    si_addr=0x9884bb8, TRAPNO=14, ERR=21, prot:3, mmaped:3
    RBX=09884bb8  EIP=09884bb8

0xecec is CheckExec's own marker, so that is Box64's bookkeeping refusing, not
a hardware fault.

Granted per page HERE, at the point of execution, not when mappings are
created. Marking guest mappings executable at mmap time - through my_mmap64 or
through setProtection_mmap on the result - makes memprot report the whole heap
as code, so the dynarec's write detection fires on ordinary heap writes; both
variants crashed the translator with no diagnostic.

PROT_NEVERCLEAN is the right flag and PROT_NOPROT is not, though both look
equivalent at the write-protection test:

  * protectDB skips a page when !(dyn & PROT_NEVERPROT), and PROT_NEVERPROT is
    (PROT_NOPROT|PROT_NEVERCLEAN), so either flag stops write-protection. That
    matters because without it the guest's next store into its own module -
    executed by the INTERPRETER, inside Run() - takes an unhandled store fault:

        unhandled signal 11 code 0x2 at 0x9800aa4 in box64[d85848,...]
        cause 0xf (store page fault), SEGV_ACCERR

    constant faulting PC, varying guest address, resolving to Run() once you
    notice box64 is non-PIE with text at 0x34800000. Box64's handler never runs
    because the PC is its own C code rather than generated code.

  * ONLY PROT_NEVERCLEAN sets always_test (dynarec_native.c:518,
    dynarec_native_pass.c:146), which re-verifies guest code before each
    execution. This game reloads modules at overlapping heap addresses
    (<<idle>> opened and closed twice, then <<phunt>>), so without always_test
    a new module can run the previous one's translation. PROT_NOPROT alone was
    measured and the game aborted on returning to the menu from a finished
    game - the exact failure always_test prevents.

  * updateProtection keys its skip on PROT_NEVERCLEAN specifically
    (`never = dyn & PROT_NEVERCLEAN`), so this flag is what that path expects.

updateProtection, not setProtection: it writes memprot only, leaving the region
type alone. setProtection would rewrite mapallmem to MEM_ALLOCATED, and
getMmapped() tests `(flag & MEM_MMAP) == MEM_MMAP`, so a still-mmapped page
would start reporting false - latent, since the only consumers are
mallochook.c's allocator dispatch under BOX64_MALLOC_HACK=2, but wrong.
updateProtection has its addDBFromAddressRange commented out, so this calls it
explicitly, matching what my_mmap64 does when a mapping becomes executable.

Gated on box64_is32bits, so 64-bit guests keep the strict check. The Wine
variant in os/emit_signal_wine.c is left alone.

NOT conditioned on PT_GNU_STACK actually being absent: Box64 parses that
segment for stack size and alignment only (elfloader.c:172) and never records
its presence, so a general version needs a new elfheader field.

--- a/src/os/emit_signals_linux.c
+++ b/src/os/emit_signals_linux.c
@@ -114,7 +114,42 @@
 {
     if (box64_pagesize != 4096)
         return; // disabling the test, 4K pagesize simlation isn't good enough for this
-    while ((getProtection/*_fast*/(addr) & (PROT_EXEC | PROT_READ)) != (PROT_EXEC | PROT_READ)) {
+    uint32_t prot;
+    while (((prot = getProtection(addr)) & (PROT_EXEC | PROT_READ)) != (PROT_EXEC | PROT_READ)) {
+        // READ_IMPLIES_EXEC, granted lazily and one page at a time.
+        //
+        // Linux sets this personality for an i386 ELF with no PT_GNU_STACK
+        // (fs/binfmt_elf.c), so readable memory is executable; pre-NX hardware
+        // behaved that way regardless. Megatouch Maxx Ruby has no PT_GNU_STACK
+        // and loads each game module into malloc'd memory, then jumps into it -
+        // which lands exactly here, with EIP==EBX==heap.
+        //
+        // PROT_NEVERCLEAN, not PROT_NOPROT. Both make protectDB skip the page
+        // (the test is `!(dyn & PROT_NEVERPROT)`), so either stops the dynarec
+        // write-protecting a page that is data AND code - without which the
+        // guest's next store into its own module, executed by the interpreter
+        // inside Run(), takes an unhandled store fault that kills the process
+        // with no diagnostic. But only PROT_NEVERCLEAN sets always_test
+        // (dynarec_native.c, dynarec_native_pass.c), which re-verifies the
+        // guest code before each execution. Without it a module reloaded at an
+        // overlapping heap address runs the PREVIOUS module's translation.
+        //
+        // updateProtection, not setProtection: it touches memprot only - its
+        // rb_set(mapallmem, ...) is commented out - so the region type is
+        // preserved. setProtection would rewrite it to MEM_ALLOCATED and make
+        // getMmapped() report false for a page that is still mmapped.
+        // updateProtection also skips addDBFromAddressRange (commented out
+        // there), so this calls it explicitly, as my_mmap64 does for any
+        // mapping that becomes executable.
+        if (box64_is32bits && (prot & PROT_READ)) {
+            uintptr_t page = addr & ~(uintptr_t)(box64_pagesize - 1);
+            updateProtection(page, box64_pagesize, prot | PROT_EXEC | PROT_NEVERCLEAN);
+#ifdef DYNAREC
+            if (BOX64ENV(dynarec))
+                addDBFromAddressRange(page, box64_pagesize);
+#endif
+            return;
+        }
         R_RIP = addr; // incase there is a slight difference
         EmitSignal(emu, X64_SIGSEGV, (void*)addr, 0xecec);
     }
